window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-06-3", "source": "2018年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of building trust between businesses and consumers. You can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Trust lies at the very heart of every healthy relationship between businesses and consumers. In an age flooded\nwith choices and advertisements, I firmly believe that mutual trust, rather than clever marketing, ultimately\ndetermines whether a company can survive and prosper.\nThe value of trust can be clearly seen through everyday examples. When a restaurant insists on fresh\ningredients and honest pricing, satisfied diners return again and again and gladly recommend it to friends, so\nthe business grows steadily. In sharp contrast, once a well-known brand is exposed for false advertising or\nhidden defects, angry consumers desert it overnight, and years of hard-won reputation collapse in a single day.\nClearly, trust is not an empty word but a real asset that either multiplies profits or, once broken, brings ruin.\nTherefore, building trust is never a burden but a wise investment for both sides. Only when businesses treat\nhonesty as a bottom line and consumers respond with loyalty can a genuine win-win situation be achieved."}, "translation": {"prompt": "自行车曾经是中国城乡最主要的交通工具，中国一度被称为\"自行车王国\"。如今，随着城市交通拥堵和空气污染日益严重，骑自行车又开始流行起来。近来，中国企业家将移动互联网技术与传统自行车结合在一起，发明了一种称为共享单车(shared bikes)的商业模式。共享单车的出现使骑车出行更加方便，人们仅需一部手机就可以随时使用共享单车。为了鼓励人们骑车出行，很多城市修建了自行车道。现在，越来越多的中国人也喜欢通过骑车健身。", "sample": "Bicycles were once the most important means of transportation in both China's cities and the countryside, and China was once called the \"Kingdom of Bicycles\". Nowadays, as traffic congestion and air pollution in cities are becoming increasingly serious, cycling is becoming popular again. Recently, Chinese entrepreneurs have invented a business model called shared bikes by combining mobile Internet technology with traditional bicycles. The emergence of shared bikes makes cycling more convenient, and people only need a mobile phone to use shared bikes at any time. To encourage people to travel by bike, many cities have built bike lanes. Nowadays, more and more Chinese people also enjoy keeping fit by cycling."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "When Elon Musk says that his new priority is using artificial intelligence to build domestic robots, we should look forward to the day in admiration.\nMr. Musk is a guy who gets things done. The founder of two tech companies, Tesla Motors and SpaceX, is bringing electric vehicles to mass market and  __26__  humans to live on other planets. This sounds like so much hot air, but the near $13 billion fortune this entrepreneur has  __27__  comes from practical achievements rather than hypothetical ones.\nA lot of clever people are  __28__  about artificial intelligence, fearing that robots will one day become so  __29__  that they'll murder all of us. These fears are mostly  __30__  : as with hysteria about genetic modification, we humans are generally wise enough to manage these problems with speed and care.\nAnd just think of how wonderful it would be if you had a live-in robot. It could,  __31__  , be like having a babysitter and a nurse rolled into one—or, if that required  __32__  intelligence beyond the power of Mr. Musk's imagined machine, at least someone to chop the carrots, wash the car and mow the lawn. Once purchased and trained, this would allow the  __33__  user to save money and time, freeing up  __34__  space in our busy lives to read a good book.\nThat is why we welcome Mr. Musk's latest  __35__  , and wish him well. As long as robots add to the sum of human happiness, reduce suffering, and create time to read world-class journalism, we should be their fans. Especially since journalism is one job robots will never do.", "matchIntro": "In the real world, nobody cares that you went to an Ivy League school", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "As a high school junior, everything in my life revolved around getting into the right college. I diligently attended my SAT, ACT, and Advanced Placement test preparation courses. I juggled(尽力应付) cross-country and track schedules, newspaper staff, and my church's youth group and drama team. I didn't drink, party, or even do much dating. The right college, I thought, was one with prestige, one with a name. It didn't have to be the Ivy League, but it needed to be a \"top school.\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "Looking back now, nine years later, I can't remember exactly what it was about these universities that made them seem so much better. Was it a curriculum that appeared more rigorous, perhaps? Or an alumni network that I hoped would open doors down the line? Maybe. \"I do think there are advantages to schools with more recognition,\" notes Marybeth Gasman, a professor of higher education at the University of Pennsylvania. \"I don't necessarily think that's a reason to go to one.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "In reflection, my firm belief in the power of the brand was naive, not to mention a bit snobby. I quickly passed over state schools and southern schools, believing their curriculums to be automatically inferior to northeastern or western counterparts. Instead, I dreamed of living in New York City and my parents obliged me with a visit to New York University's (NYU) campus. During the tour, tuition fees were discussed. (NYU is consistently ranked one of the country's most expensive schools, with room and board costs totaling upwards of $64,000 a year.) Up until then, I hadn't truly realized just how expensive an education can be. Over the next few months, I realized not only could I not afford my dream school, I couldn't even afford the ones where I'd been accepted. City University of New York (CUNY), Rutgers University, and Indiana University were out of reach as were Mississippi State and the University of Alabama, where I would have to pay out-of-state fees. Further complicating my college search was a flourishing track career—I wanted to keep running but my times weren't quite fast enough to secure a scholarship."}, {"label": "D", "text": "And so, at 11 pm on the night of Georgia State University's (GSU) midnight deadline, I applied online. Rated No. 466 overall on Forbes' Lists Top Colleges, No. 183 in Research Universities, and No. 108 in the South, I can't say it was my top choice. Still, the track coach had offered me a walk-on spot, and I actually found the urban Atlanta campus a decent consolation prize after New York City."}, {"label": "E", "text": "While it may have been practical, it wasn't prestigious. But here's the thing: I loved my \"lower-tier\"(低层次的) university. (I use the term \"low-tier\" cautiously, because GSU is a well-regarded research institution that attracts high quality professors and faculty from all over the country.) We are taught to believe that only by going to the best schools and getting the best grades can we escape the rat race and build a better future. But what if lower-tier colleges and universities were the ticket to escaping the rat race? After all, where else can you leave school with a decent degree—but without a lifetime of debt?"}, {"label": "F", "text": "My school didn't come prepackaged like the more popular options, so we were left to take care of ourselves, figuring out city life and trying to complete degree programs that no one was championing for us to succeed in. What I'm saying is, I loved my university because it taught us all to be resourceful and we could make what we wanted out of it."}, {"label": "G", "text": "I was lucky enough to have my tuition covered by a lottery-funded scholarship called HOPE (Helping Outstanding Pupils Educationally). When I started college, the HOPE scholarship was funded by the state of Georgia and offered to graduating high school seniors with a GPA of 3.0 or higher. Living costs and books I paid for with money earned during high school, supplemented by a small college fund my deceased grandfather left for me and a modest savings account my parents created when I was born."}, {"label": "H", "text": "So what about all that name recognition? Sure, many of my colleagues and competitors have more glamorous alma maters(母校) than I do. As a journalist, I have competed against NYU, Columbia, and Northeastern graduates for jobs. And yet, not a single interviewer has ever asked me about my educational background. In fact, almost every interview I've ever had was due to a connection—one that I've gained through pure determination, not a school brand."}, {"label": "I", "text": "According to The Boston Globe, students who earned their bachelor's in 2012 have an average monthly loan payment of $312, which is one-third more than those who graduated in 2004. Ultimately, that's the thing universities don't want to admit. Private universities are money-making institutions. If you can afford to buy prestige, that's your choice. For the rest of us, however, our hearty lower-tiered universities are just fine, thank you."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Wealthy universities talk up the benefits their name will give graduates: namely, strong alumni networks, star faculty, and a résumé boost. But you needn't attend an Ivy League school to reap those rewards. Ludacris and the former CEO of Bank of America Ken Lewis are alumni of my college, as well as VICE's first female editor-in-chief, Ellis Jones. Successful people tend to be successful no matter where they go to school, and lower-tier schools can have alumni networks just as strong as their big name counterparts. In fact, lower-tier school alumni networks are arguably stronger, because fellow alumni recognize that you didn't necessarily have an easy path to follow. They might be more willing to offer career help, because your less famous school denotes that, like them, you are also full of energy and perseverance."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The Washington Post reported on a recent study by Princeton economists, in which college graduates who applied to the most selective schools in the 12th grade were compared to those who applied to slightly less selective schools. They found that students with more potential earned more as adults, and the reverse held true as well, no matter where they went to school."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Likewise, star faculty are not always found where you'd expect. Big name schools are not necessarily the best places for professors; plus, many professors split teaching time between multiple colleges and/or universities. This means, for instance, a CUNY student could reasonably expect to receive the same quality of instruction from a prestigious professor as they would if they were enrolled in the same class at NYU."}, {"label": "M", "text": "It's possible that some hiring managers may be drawn to candidates with a particular educational résumé, but it's no guarantee. According to a 2012 survey described in The Atlantic, college reputation ranked lowest in relative importance of attributes in evaluating graduates for hire, beaten out by top factors like internships, employment during college, college major, volunteer experience, and extracurriculars."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Maybe students who choose less prestigious universities are bound to succeed because they are determined to. I tend to think so. In any case, if I could do it again, I'd still make the same choice. Today I'm debt-free, resourceful—and I understand that even the shiniest packaging can't predict what you'll find on the inside."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Economically speaking, are we better off than we were ten years ago? Twenty years ago?\nIn their thirst for evidence on this issue, commentators seized on the recent report by the Census Bureau, which found that average household income rose by 5.2% in 2015. Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete, statistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it excludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2) it is based on surveys rather than data. Even if precisely measured, income data exclude important determinants of economic well-being, such as the hours of work needed to earn that income.\nWhile thinking about the question, we came across a recently published article by Charles Jones and Peter Klenow, which proposes an interesting new measure of economic welfare. While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income, taking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working time, life expectancy, and inequality. Moreover, it can be used to assess economic performance both across countries and over time.\nThe Jones-Klenow method can be illustrated by a cross-country example. Suppose we want to compare the economic welfare of citizens of the U.S. and France in 2005.\nIn 2005, as the authors observe: real consumption per person in France was only 60% as high as the U.S., making it appear that Americans were economically much better off than the French on average. However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality. The French take longer vacations and retire earlier, so typically work fewer hours; they enjoy a higher life expectancy, presumably reflecting advantages with respect to health care, diet, lifestyle, and the like; and income and consumption are somewhat more equally distributed there than in the U.S. Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with the U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\nSimilar calculations can be used to compare the U.S. and other countries. For example, this calculation puts economic welfare in the United Kingdom at 97% of U.S. levels, but estimates Mexican well-being at 22%.\nThe Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time. According to this measure, as of the early-to-mid-2000s, the U.S. had the highest economic welfare of any large country. Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of improvement has slowed markedly.\nMethodologically, the lesson from the Jones-Klenow research is that economic welfare is multi-dimensional. Their approach is flexible enough that in principle other important quality-of-life changes could be incorporated-for example, decreases in total emissions of pollutants and declines in crime rates.", "47": "Economically speaking, are we better off than we were ten years ago? Twenty years ago?\nIn their thirst for evidence on this issue, commentators seized on the recent report by the Census Bureau, which found that average household income rose by 5.2% in 2015. Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete, statistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it excludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2) it is based on surveys rather than data. Even if precisely measured, income data exclude important determinants of economic well-being, such as the hours of work needed to earn that income.\nWhile thinking about the question, we came across a recently published article by Charles Jones and Peter Klenow, which proposes an interesting new measure of economic welfare. While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income, taking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working time, life expectancy, and inequality. Moreover, it can be used to assess economic performance both across countries and over time.\nThe Jones-Klenow method can be illustrated by a cross-country example. Suppose we want to compare the economic welfare of citizens of the U.S. and France in 2005.\nIn 2005, as the authors observe: real consumption per person in France was only 60% as high as the U.S., making it appear that Americans were economically much better off than the French on average. However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality. The French take longer vacations and retire earlier, so typically work fewer hours; they enjoy a higher life expectancy, presumably reflecting advantages with respect to health care, diet, lifestyle, and the like; and income and consumption are somewhat more equally distributed there than in the U.S. Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with the U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\nSimilar calculations can be used to compare the U.S. and other countries. For example, this calculation puts economic welfare in the United Kingdom at 97% of U.S. levels, but estimates Mexican well-being at 22%.\nThe Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time. According to this measure, as of the early-to-mid-2000s, the U.S. had the highest economic welfare of any large country. Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of improvement has slowed markedly.\nMethodologically, the lesson from the Jones-Klenow research is that economic welfare is multi-dimensional. Their approach is flexible enough that in principle other important quality-of-life changes could be incorporated-for example, decreases in total emissions of pollutants and declines in crime rates.", "48": "Economically speaking, are we better off than we were ten years ago? Twenty years ago?\nIn their thirst for evidence on this issue, commentators seized on the recent report by the Census Bureau, which found that average household income rose by 5.2% in 2015. Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete, statistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it excludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2) it is based on surveys rather than data. Even if precisely measured, income data exclude important determinants of economic well-being, such as the hours of work needed to earn that income.\nWhile thinking about the question, we came across a recently published article by Charles Jones and Peter Klenow, which proposes an interesting new measure of economic welfare. While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income, taking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working time, life expectancy, and inequality. Moreover, it can be used to assess economic performance both across countries and over time.\nThe Jones-Klenow method can be illustrated by a cross-country example. Suppose we want to compare the economic welfare of citizens of the U.S. and France in 2005.\nIn 2005, as the authors observe: real consumption per person in France was only 60% as high as the U.S., making it appear that Americans were economically much better off than the French on average. However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality. The French take longer vacations and retire earlier, so typically work fewer hours; they enjoy a higher life expectancy, presumably reflecting advantages with respect to health care, diet, lifestyle, and the like; and income and consumption are somewhat more equally distributed there than in the U.S. Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with the U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\nSimilar calculations can be used to compare the U.S. and other countries. For example, this calculation puts economic welfare in the United Kingdom at 97% of U.S. levels, but estimates Mexican well-being at 22%.\nThe Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time. According to this measure, as of the early-to-mid-2000s, the U.S. had the highest economic welfare of any large country. Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of improvement has slowed markedly.\nMethodologically, the lesson from the Jones-Klenow research is that economic welfare is multi-dimensional. Their approach is flexible enough that in principle other important quality-of-life changes could be incorporated-for example, decreases in total emissions of pollutants and declines in crime rates.", "49": "Economically speaking, are we better off than we were ten years ago? Twenty years ago?\nIn their thirst for evidence on this issue, commentators seized on the recent report by the Census Bureau, which found that average household income rose by 5.2% in 2015. Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete, statistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it excludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2) it is based on surveys rather than data. Even if precisely measured, income data exclude important determinants of economic well-being, such as the hours of work needed to earn that income.\nWhile thinking about the question, we came across a recently published article by Charles Jones and Peter Klenow, which proposes an interesting new measure of economic welfare. While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income, taking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working time, life expectancy, and inequality. Moreover, it can be used to assess economic performance both across countries and over time.\nThe Jones-Klenow method can be illustrated by a cross-country example. Suppose we want to compare the economic welfare of citizens of the U.S. and France in 2005.\nIn 2005, as the authors observe: real consumption per person in France was only 60% as high as the U.S., making it appear that Americans were economically much better off than the French on average. However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality. The French take longer vacations and retire earlier, so typically work fewer hours; they enjoy a higher life expectancy, presumably reflecting advantages with respect to health care, diet, lifestyle, and the like; and income and consumption are somewhat more equally distributed there than in the U.S. Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with the U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\nSimilar calculations can be used to compare the U.S. and other countries. For example, this calculation puts economic welfare in the United Kingdom at 97% of U.S. levels, but estimates Mexican well-being at 22%.\nThe Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time. According to this measure, as of the early-to-mid-2000s, the U.S. had the highest economic welfare of any large country. Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of improvement has slowed markedly.\nMethodologically, the lesson from the Jones-Klenow research is that economic welfare is multi-dimensional. Their approach is flexible enough that in principle other important quality-of-life changes could be incorporated-for example, decreases in total emissions of pollutants and declines in crime rates.", "50": "Economically speaking, are we better off than we were ten years ago? Twenty years ago?\nIn their thirst for evidence on this issue, commentators seized on the recent report by the Census Bureau, which found that average household income rose by 5.2% in 2015. Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete, statistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it excludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2) it is based on surveys rather than data. Even if precisely measured, income data exclude important determinants of economic well-being, such as the hours of work needed to earn that income.\nWhile thinking about the question, we came across a recently published article by Charles Jones and Peter Klenow, which proposes an interesting new measure of economic welfare. While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income, taking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working time, life expectancy, and inequality. Moreover, it can be used to assess economic performance both across countries and over time.\nThe Jones-Klenow method can be illustrated by a cross-country example. Suppose we want to compare the economic welfare of citizens of the U.S. and France in 2005.\nIn 2005, as the authors observe: real consumption per person in France was only 60% as high as the U.S., making it appear that Americans were economically much better off than the French on average. However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality. The French take longer vacations and retire earlier, so typically work fewer hours; they enjoy a higher life expectancy, presumably reflecting advantages with respect to health care, diet, lifestyle, and the like; and income and consumption are somewhat more equally distributed there than in the U.S. Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with the U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\nSimilar calculations can be used to compare the U.S. and other countries. For example, this calculation puts economic welfare in the United Kingdom at 97% of U.S. levels, but estimates Mexican well-being at 22%.\nThe Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time. According to this measure, as of the early-to-mid-2000s, the U.S. had the highest economic welfare of any large country. Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of improvement has slowed markedly.\nMethodologically, the lesson from the Jones-Klenow research is that economic welfare is multi-dimensional. Their approach is flexible enough that in principle other important quality-of-life changes could be incorporated-for example, decreases in total emissions of pollutants and declines in crime rates.", "51": "If you've ever started a sentence with, \"If I were you…\" or found yourself scratching your head at a colleague's agony over a decision when the answer is crystal-clear, there's a scientific reason behind it. Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing indecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that doesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\nThe problem is \"decision fatigue,\" a psychological phenomenon that takes a toll on the quality of your choices after a long day of decision making, says Evan Polman, a leading psychologist.\nPhysicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple and easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate further,\" Polman says.\nBut decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else. When people imagine themselves as advisers and imagine their own choices as belonging to someone else, they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts to make those choices. \"By taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the consequences of decision fatigue,\" he says. \"It's as if there's something fun and liberating about making someone else's choice.\"\nGetting input from others not only offers a fresh perspective and thought process; it often also includes riskier choices. While this sounds undesirable, it can be quite good, says Polman. \"When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo(现状),\" he says. \"But the status quo can be problematic, since a change in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\"\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential. \"People who are susceptible to decision fatigue will likely choose to do nothing over something,\" he says, \"That's not to say that risk is always good, but it is related to taking action whereas decision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction and the possible chagrin(懊恼) of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new course but is unfortunately hindered.\"\nJust because you can make good choices for others doesn't mean you'll do the same for yourself, Polman cautions. \"Research has found that women negotiate higher salaries for others than they do for themselves,\" he says, adding that people slip in and out of decision roles.", "52": "If you've ever started a sentence with, \"If I were you…\" or found yourself scratching your head at a colleague's agony over a decision when the answer is crystal-clear, there's a scientific reason behind it. Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing indecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that doesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\nThe problem is \"decision fatigue,\" a psychological phenomenon that takes a toll on the quality of your choices after a long day of decision making, says Evan Polman, a leading psychologist.\nPhysicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple and easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate further,\" Polman says.\nBut decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else. When people imagine themselves as advisers and imagine their own choices as belonging to someone else, they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts to make those choices. \"By taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the consequences of decision fatigue,\" he says. \"It's as if there's something fun and liberating about making someone else's choice.\"\nGetting input from others not only offers a fresh perspective and thought process; it often also includes riskier choices. While this sounds undesirable, it can be quite good, says Polman. \"When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo(现状),\" he says. \"But the status quo can be problematic, since a change in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\"\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential. \"People who are susceptible to decision fatigue will likely choose to do nothing over something,\" he says, \"That's not to say that risk is always good, but it is related to taking action whereas decision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction and the possible chagrin(懊恼) of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new course but is unfortunately hindered.\"\nJust because you can make good choices for others doesn't mean you'll do the same for yourself, Polman cautions. \"Research has found that women negotiate higher salaries for others than they do for themselves,\" he says, adding that people slip in and out of decision roles.", "53": "If you've ever started a sentence with, \"If I were you…\" or found yourself scratching your head at a colleague's agony over a decision when the answer is crystal-clear, there's a scientific reason behind it. Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing indecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that doesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\nThe problem is \"decision fatigue,\" a psychological phenomenon that takes a toll on the quality of your choices after a long day of decision making, says Evan Polman, a leading psychologist.\nPhysicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple and easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate further,\" Polman says.\nBut decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else. When people imagine themselves as advisers and imagine their own choices as belonging to someone else, they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts to make those choices. \"By taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the consequences of decision fatigue,\" he says. \"It's as if there's something fun and liberating about making someone else's choice.\"\nGetting input from others not only offers a fresh perspective and thought process; it often also includes riskier choices. While this sounds undesirable, it can be quite good, says Polman. \"When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo(现状),\" he says. \"But the status quo can be problematic, since a change in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\"\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential. \"People who are susceptible to decision fatigue will likely choose to do nothing over something,\" he says, \"That's not to say that risk is always good, but it is related to taking action whereas decision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction and the possible chagrin(懊恼) of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new course but is unfortunately hindered.\"\nJust because you can make good choices for others doesn't mean you'll do the same for yourself, Polman cautions. \"Research has found that women negotiate higher salaries for others than they do for themselves,\" he says, adding that people slip in and out of decision roles.", "54": "If you've ever started a sentence with, \"If I were you…\" or found yourself scratching your head at a colleague's agony over a decision when the answer is crystal-clear, there's a scientific reason behind it. Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing indecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that doesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\nThe problem is \"decision fatigue,\" a psychological phenomenon that takes a toll on the quality of your choices after a long day of decision making, says Evan Polman, a leading psychologist.\nPhysicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple and easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate further,\" Polman says.\nBut decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else. When people imagine themselves as advisers and imagine their own choices as belonging to someone else, they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts to make those choices. \"By taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the consequences of decision fatigue,\" he says. \"It's as if there's something fun and liberating about making someone else's choice.\"\nGetting input from others not only offers a fresh perspective and thought process; it often also includes riskier choices. While this sounds undesirable, it can be quite good, says Polman. \"When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo(现状),\" he says. \"But the status quo can be problematic, since a change in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\"\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential. \"People who are susceptible to decision fatigue will likely choose to do nothing over something,\" he says, \"That's not to say that risk is always good, but it is related to taking action whereas decision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction and the possible chagrin(懊恼) of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new course but is unfortunately hindered.\"\nJust because you can make good choices for others doesn't mean you'll do the same for yourself, Polman cautions. \"Research has found that women negotiate higher salaries for others than they do for themselves,\" he says, adding that people slip in and out of decision roles.", "55": "If you've ever started a sentence with, \"If I were you…\" or found yourself scratching your head at a colleague's agony over a decision when the answer is crystal-clear, there's a scientific reason behind it. Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing indecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that doesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\nThe problem is \"decision fatigue,\" a psychological phenomenon that takes a toll on the quality of your choices after a long day of decision making, says Evan Polman, a leading psychologist.\nPhysicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple and easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate further,\" Polman says.\nBut decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else. When people imagine themselves as advisers and imagine their own choices as belonging to someone else, they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts to make those choices. \"By taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the consequences of decision fatigue,\" he says. \"It's as if there's something fun and liberating about making someone else's choice.\"\nGetting input from others not only offers a fresh perspective and thought process; it often also includes riskier choices. While this sounds undesirable, it can be quite good, says Polman. \"When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo(现状),\" he says. \"But the status quo can be problematic, since a change in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\"\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential. \"People who are susceptible to decision fatigue will likely choose to do nothing over something,\" he says, \"That's not to say that risk is always good, but it is related to taking action whereas decision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction and the possible chagrin(懊恼) of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new course but is unfortunately hindered.\"\nJust because you can make good choices for others doesn't mean you'll do the same for yourself, Polman cautions. \"Research has found that women negotiate higher salaries for others than they do for themselves,\" he says, adding that people slip in and out of decision roles."}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) enabling\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列谓语，需现在分词形式\n【依据】enable sb. to do 锁死\nand __(26)__ humans to live on other planets结构上正是 enable sb. to do sth.，意为\n『让人类能在其他星球生活』，与上文『把电动车带进大众市场』并列，描绘马斯克的两项\n实际推进。\n【竞争词】A) amassed 形态与搭配双错\namassed 是过去分词形式，无法与前面的 is bringing 并列；且 amass 意为『积累』，不接 sb.\nto do，语义和结构都不通。\nF) exaggerated 形态错且语义不通\nexaggerated 同样是过去分词形式，难与 is bringing 形成并列主动义；意思是『夸大』，不与\n『让人类在其他星球生活』的语义对应。\nI) misleading 感情色彩相反\nmisleading 是『误导』，全文对马斯克持正面态度（we should look forward to the day in\nadmiration.），说他『误导人类去其他星球生活』方向相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) amassed\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句缺谓语，需过去分词或过去式\n【依据】amass a fortune 是固定搭配\namass 与 fortune/wealth 搭配表示『积聚财富』，正是the near $13 billion fortune this\nentrepreneur has __(27)__要表达的『这位企业家所积聚的近 130 亿美元财富』。\n【竞争词】F) exaggerated 语义与下文矛盾\nexaggerated 意为『夸大』，会变成『这位企业家所夸大的财富』，与后文comes from\npractical achievements rather than hypothetical ones.强调其『真实成就』的正面评价直接\n相悖。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) terrified\n形容词\n【词性槽】be + ___ + about 是表语形容词位\n【依据】fearing that 是同位重述\n紧跟的fearing that robots will one day become so __(29)__ that they'll murder all of\nus.用 fear 同义复述空格里的情绪，terrified（感到恐惧）正与 fear 同源同义；且 be\nterrified about 是地道搭配。\n【竞争词】C) emotional 情绪笼统、不指向恐惧\nemotional 表『情绪化的』，无明确恐惧含义，与 fearing 的对应不上；且 be emotional\nabout 也不能精准对应『因 AI 而害怕』。\nB) casual 态度方向相反\ncasual 表『随意的、漫不经心的』，与 fearing 所体现的高度担心完全相反。\nG) extravagant 形容铺张、不形容情绪\nextravagant 多形容花费/言行『铺张过度』，不形容人对某事的情绪状态。\nJ) precious 词义错位\nprecious 表『珍贵的』，与『about artificial intelligence』和 fearing 都搭不上线。\nL) smart 评价聪明而非情绪\nsmart 是『聪明的』，说『clever people are smart about AI』既同义重复 clever 又与下文\nfearing 衔接不顺。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) smart\n形容词\n【词性槽】so + ___ + that 程度结构需形容词\n【依据】AI 担忧的核心是『太聪明』\n全段都在谈 AI（A lot of clever people are __(28)__ about artificial intelligence）的『过\n分聪明』之忧——机器人变得如此 smart 以至于会反过来杀人，逻辑自洽。\n【竞争词】C) emotional 机器人会因情感化而杀人不成立\nemotional 强调情感化，文中并未把机器人的威胁归因于『有情感』，而是归因于智能过强。\nB) casual 语义方向不对\ncasual（随意的）不构成『杀掉我们』的合理原因。\nG) extravagant 搭配语义不通\nextravagant（铺张的）不修饰机器人的能力，且与 murder 的因果不通。\nJ) precious 感情色彩相反\nprecious 是褒义的『珍贵』，与『杀掉我们』的可怕后果在感情色彩上完全冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) exaggerated\n形容词\n【词性槽】These fears are mostly ___ 表语位\n【依据】冒号后内容是『被夸大』的同义重述\nas with hysteria about genetic modification, we humans are generally wise enough\nto manage these problems with speed and care.——把对 AI 的担忧类比为转基因恐\n慌，并说人类有能力处理，等于在说这些担忧『言过其实』，exaggerated 正合此意。\n【竞争词】I) misleading 义近但侧重不同\nmisleading 强调『有意/无意误导他人』，更指向信息源的错误；本句强调担忧本身被放大、并\n非他人在误导，所以语义偏差。\nC) emotional 未点中『过度』\nemotional（情绪化）只是说反应带情绪，不能像 exaggerated 那样直接对应冒号后的『其实\n人类能轻松搞定』。\nG) extravagant 搭配不自然\nextravagant 多形容花费或言行铺张，用来评价 fears 不是地道搭配，且语义焦点跑偏。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— exaggerated 与 misleading 同义近形。These fears are\nmostly __(30)__两个词都能放进表语位且都带『不实』含义，主体考生很容易选\nmisleading；防坑——misleading 强调『误导他人』，需有信息源；本句焦点是担忧本身\n被放大，应用 exaggerated。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) eventually\n副词\n【词性槽】It could, ___, be 句中插入位\n【依据】对应『终有一天』的设想\nIt could, __(31)__, be like having a babysitter and a nurse rolled into one是在做未来设\n想——『它终究/最终可能像保姆兼护士的合体』，eventually 与 could 联用表示时间推移\n后的可能结果，最自然。\n【竞争词】H) generously 修饰对象错位\ngenerously 是『慷慨地』，多修饰给予/帮助类动作；说『它慷慨地像保姆和护士的合体』在搭\n配和逻辑上都说不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) emotional\n形容词\n【词性槽】required + ___ + intelligence 名词前定语位\n【依据】emotional intelligence 是固定表达\nif that required __(32)__ intelligence beyond the power of Mr. Musk's imagined\nmachine想说『如果这超出了马斯克想象的机器的能力（一种机器难以拥有的智能）』，\nemotional intelligence『情商』正是公认机器难以达到的能力，与上文 babysitter and\nnurse 的人际照护属性高度契合。\n【竞争词】G) extravagant 搭配不通\nextravagant intelligence 不是常见搭配，且 extravagant 偏『铺张浪费』的负面色彩，与本段\n轻松正面的口吻不合。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— extravagant intelligence 似通实不通。required __(32)__\nintelligence beyond the power of Mr. Musk's imagined machine中，extravagant 看似\n也能表达『超出常规的智能』，但 extravagant intelligence 不是地道搭配；防坑——优先\n想 emotional intelligence 这一公认术语，并且 extravagant 的负面色彩与本段轻松假设\n语境不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) casual\n形容词\n【词性槽】the + ___ + user 是名词前定语位\n【依据】对应『普通的家用机器人用户』\n本段从And just think of how wonderful it would be if you had a live-in robot.起，假\n想的是普通家庭里随便买一台用的用户，casual user 即『普通/非专业用户』，与让其『省\n钱省时、有空读书』的口吻一致。\n【竞争词】C) emotional 与省钱省时无关\nemotional user 是『情绪化用户』，与下文 save money and time 的功利收益对不上。\nG) extravagant 感情色彩偏负面\nextravagant 暗含『挥霍』，与紧跟的 save money（省钱）正面收益逻辑直接相反。\nJ) precious 搭配错位\nprecious user 不是地道搭配，且 precious 更典型地形容时间/空间，后面 34 题正好是\nprecious space 的归属。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) precious\n形容词\n【词性槽】freeing up + ___ + space 名词前定语位\n【依据】precious space 是地道搭配\nprecious 与 time/space 搭配是英语里最常见的『宝贵的时间/空间』表达；这里freeing\nup __(34)__ space in our busy lives to read a good book.意思是『在繁忙生活中腾出宝\n贵的空间来读本好书』，逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】C) emotional 搭配不通\nemotional space（情绪空间）虽存在，但与 freeing up… to read a good book 的生活时间/\n空间安排语境不合，且过于文艺。\nG) extravagant 感情色彩反向\nextravagant space 暗含『过度浪费的空间』，与『腾出空间』所暗含的『珍贵不舍』正好\n相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) amassed", "B) casual", "C) emotional", "D) enabling", "E) eventually", "F) exaggerated", "G) extravagant", "H) generously", "I) misleading", "J) precious", "K) reward", "L) smart", "M) sphere", "N) terrified", "O) venture"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) venture\n名词\n【词性槽】Mr. Musk's latest ___ 名词性中心词\n【依据】回扣开篇『new priority』\n全文的引子When Elon Musk says that his new priority is using artificial intelligence\nto build domestic robots说的就是马斯克的新事业，末段we welcome Mr. Musk's latest\n__(35)__显然要用一个名词指代这件『新事业』，venture（创新/冒险性事业）正好对应。\n【竞争词】K) reward 回报不是『举措』\nreward 是某行动之后得到的『回报』，不能用来指代马斯克自己发起的新动作，与 welcome、\nwish him well 的指向对不上。\nM) sphere 领域不能被 welcome\nsphere 是『领域』，是抽象范围，不是具体动作或项目；用 welcome his latest sphere\n不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Modest institutions can also have successful graduates and strong alumni networks.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】普通学校也有成功校友与强校友网\n【定位】Successful people tend to be successful no matter where they go to school, and lower-tier\nschools can have alumni networks just as strong as their big name counterparts.\n成功的人不管上哪所学校都会成功，而层次较低的学校也能拥有与名校一样强大的校友\n网络。\n【改写】Modest institutions can also have successful graduates ↔\nSuccessful people tend to be successful no matter where they go to school\nModest institutions ≈ lower-tier schools\nstrong alumni networks ↔ alumni networks just as strong as their big name counterparts\nas strong as 名校 = strong alumni networks\n【辨邻】 K K 段聚焦 students with more potential earned more as adults 这条关于个人潜力\n的结论，没有一句涉及 strong alumni networks，只对上 successful graduates 的\n一半，因此不是源头段。\nstudents with more potential earned more as adults, and the reverse held true as well, no matter\nwhere they went to school", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The money the author made in high school helped pay for her living expenses and books at college.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高中赚的钱付生活费和书本\n【定位】Living costs and books I paid for with money earned during high school, supplemented by a\nsmall college fund my deceased grandfather left for me and a modest savings account my\nparents created when I was born.\n生活费和书本是用我高中时挣的钱支付的，再加上已故祖父留给我的一小笔大学基金以及父\n母在我出生时为我设立的一小笔储蓄账户。\n【改写】money the author made in high school ↔ money earned during high school\nmade ≈ earned\nliving expenses and books ↔ Living costs and books\nliving expenses ≈ Living costs", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The author came to see how costly college education could be when she was trying to choose a university to attend.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】选校时才意识到大学多贵\n【定位】Up until then, I hadn't truly realized just how expensive an education can be.\n在那之前，我没有真正意识到教育能有多昂贵。\n【改写】came to see how costly college education could be ↔\nI hadn't truly realized just how expensive an education can be\ncame to see = 之前没意识到 转 开始意识到\nwhen she was trying to choose a university to attend ↔\nOver the next few months, I realized not only could I not afford my dream school, I couldn't\neven afford the ones where I'd been accepted\n选校情境的概括\n【辨邻】 I I 段聚焦 average monthly loan payment of $312 这类 已经毕业的人还贷 的事实，\n没有任何 选校过程中意识到学费贵 的顿悟情境，故不是本题源头。\nstudents who earned their bachelor's in 2012 have an average monthly loan payment of $312", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "A recent study found that a graduate's salary is determined by their potential, not the university they attended.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】最近一项研究+潜力决定收入\n【定位】They found that students with more potential earned more as adults, and the reverse held true\nas well, no matter where they went to school.\n他们发现潜力更大的学生成年后赚得更多，反之亦然，无论他们上的是哪所学校。\n【改写】A recent study ↔ a recent study by Princeton economists\na recent study 原样复现\na graduate's salary is determined by their potential ↔\nstudents with more potential earned more as adults\nearned more 名词化为 salary determined by\nnot the university they attended ↔ no matter where they went to school\n让步状语 ↔ 否定并列\n【辨邻】 M M 段的 2012 survey described in The Atlantic 给出的是 college reputation\nranked lowest in relative importance of attributes in evaluating graduates for\nhire 这种 招聘官在评估时如何排序 的结果，而题干问的是 salary 是被什么决定，因\n变量不同，故排除。\nAccording to a 2012 survey described in The Atlantic, college reputation ranked lowest in relative\nimportance of attributes in evaluating graduates for hire", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The author cannot recall for sure what made certain top universities appear a lot better.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】记不清名校到底好在哪\n【定位】Looking back now, nine years later, I can't remember exactly what it was about these\nuniversities that made them seem so much better.\n如今九年后回望，我已经记不太清究竟是名校的什么让它们当初看起来好这么多。\n【改写】cannot recall for sure ↔ can't remember exactly\nrecall ≈ remember；for sure ≈ exactly\nwhat made certain top universities appear a lot better ↔\nwhat it was about these universities that made them seem so much better\nappear ≈ seem；a lot better ≈ so much better\n【辨邻】 A A 段强调当年 The right college, I thought, was one with prestige 这种 当时的笃\n信，与 现在记不清 的回望视角时间方向相反，故排除。\nThe right college, I thought, was one with prestige, one with a name", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "None of the author's job interviewers cared which college she went to.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】面试官从不问她上的哪所大学\n【定位】And yet, not a single interviewer has ever asked me about my educational background.\n然而，没有一个面试官问过我的教育背景。\n【改写】None of the author's job interviewers ↔ not a single interviewer\nNone ≈ not a single\ncared which college she went to ↔ asked me about my educational background\n从未问起 → 不在意\n【辨邻】 M M 段用 2012 survey described in The Atlantic 调查给出招聘官对学校声誉的平均排\n序，是 第三方统计视角；题干问的是 作者自己 的面试官，叙述视角不同，故排除。\nIt's possible that some hiring managers may be drawn to candidates with a particular educational\nrésumé, but it's no guarantee", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The author thinks she did the right thing in choosing a less prestigious university.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】回头看选不显赫的大学做对了\n【定位】In any case, if I could do it again, I'd still make the same choice.\n无论如何，如果让我再选一次，我还是会做同样的选择。\n【改写】did the right thing in choosing a less prestigious university ↔\nif I could do it again, I'd still make the same choice\n再选还是同样 = 当初选对了\nless prestigious university ↔ students who choose less prestigious universities\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 E E 段的核心情感是 I loved my \"lower-tier\" university，属于 喜欢；它不包含 did the\nright thing 这种 回望评价 的视角，故不是源头段（Q44 才是给 E 的题）。\nI loved my \"lower-tier\" university\nF F 段强调 to be resourceful 这种学校带给她的成长体验，是 收获 而非 评价当年选择\n对错，故排除。\nI loved my university because it taught us all to be resourceful and we could make what we wanted\nout of it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "In order to be admitted to a prestigious university, the author took part in various extracurricular activities and attended test preparation courses.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】考前培训+各种课外活动\n【定位】I diligently attended my SAT, ACT, and Advanced Placement test preparation courses. I\njuggled(尽力应付) cross-country and track schedules, newspaper staff, and my church's\nyouth group and drama team.\n我勤勤恳恳地上 SAT、ACT 和 AP 备考课程。我尽力应付越野跑和田径训练时间表、校报工\n作以及教会的青年团和戏剧团。\n【改写】attended test preparation courses ↔\nattended my SAT, ACT, and Advanced Placement test preparation courses\ntest preparation courses 原样复现\nvarious extracurricular activities ↔\ncross-country and track schedules, newspaper staff, and my church's youth group and drama\nteam\n具体活动清单 → extracurricular activities 上位\nIn order to be admitted to a prestigious university ↔\neverything in my life revolved around getting into the right college\n目的状语 + 名校替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The author liked her university which was not prestigious but less expensive.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】喜欢这所不显赫但更便宜的学校\n【定位】While it may have been practical, it wasn't prestigious. But here's the thing: I loved my\n\"lower-tier\" university.\n虽然它可能很实用，但并不显赫。然而事情是这样的：我爱我这所 低层次 的大学。\n【改写】liked her university ↔ I loved my \"lower-tier\" university\nliked ≈ loved\nnot prestigious ↔ it wasn't prestigious\nwasn't prestigious 原样复现\nless expensive ↔\nwhere else can you leave school with a decent degree—but without a lifetime of debt?\n不背一辈子债 = 更便宜\n【辨邻】 F F 段 I loved my university because it taught us all to be resourceful 强调的是 学\n校教自力更生 这一价值，而题干约束是 不显赫但更便宜 的属性组合，重点对不上，故\n排除。\nI loved my university because it taught us all to be resourceful and we could make what we wanted\nout of it\nN N 段 if I could do it again, I'd still make the same choice 是 回望评价，不是直接\n喜欢这所学校 的当下情感表态，故排除（它是 Q42 的源头）。\nif I could do it again, I'd still make the same choice", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Colleges are reluctant to admit that graduates today are in heavier debt.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大学不愿承认毕业生背更重的债\n【定位】Ultimately, that's the thing universities don't want to admit.\n归根结底，这就是大学不愿承认的事情。\n【改写】Colleges are reluctant to admit ↔ that's the thing universities don't want to admit\nColleges ≈ universities；reluctant ≈ don't want\ngraduates today are in heavier debt ↔\nstudents who earned their bachelor's in 2012 have an average monthly loan payment of $312,\nwhich is one-third more than those who graduated in 2004\n比 2004 多三分之一 = 债更重\n【辨邻】 C C 段 I hadn't truly realized just how expensive an education can be 谈的是作者本\n人 的顿悟，没有 大学不愿承认 这一态度指纹，故排除。\nUp until then, I hadn't truly realized just how expensive an education can be", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author think of the 2015 report by the Census Bureau?", "options": ["A) It is based on questionable statistics.", "B) It reflects the economic changes.", "C) It evidences the improved welfare.", "D) It provides much food for thought."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think of the 2015 report by the Census Bureau? 明确指向\n一个具体对象（2015 年报告），答案落在该对象出现处一两句之内，属于带态度色彩的细\n节题，而非主旨。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the 2015 report by the Census Bureau? 是标准 wh- 疑\n问句，think of =「对……怎么看」，of 后接评价对象。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete,\nstatistic. Among the more significant problems with the Census's measure are that: 1) it\nexcludes taxes, transfers, and compensation like employer-provided health insurance; and 2)\nit is based on surveys rather than data.\n遗憾的是，那个结论过分倚重了一种虽有用、但有缺陷且不完整的统计数据。人口普查局这\n项统计的主要问题是：1）它不包含税收、转移支付以及雇主提供的健康保险等补偿；2）它\n基于的是调查而非数据。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nflawed and incomplete, statistic ↔ questionable statistics\nit is based on surveys rather than data ↔ questionable statistics\n预判「数据/统计有缺陷」与 questionable statistics 是同一极性的同义改写，A 即正\n确项。\nB) 与原文相反\n选项称报告 reflects the economic changes，把作者的否定评价说成了中性甚至肯定\n的事实陈述，与 flawed and incomplete 极性相反。\nC) 与原文相反\nevidences the improved welfare 是 average household income rose by 5.2% in\n2015 这一表象给人的直观结论，但作者随即用 Unfortunately 否定了这个结论。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有任何句子表明该报告 provides much food for thought（给人很多启迪/可思\n考之处）；作者只指出它有缺陷，并未夸其引人深思。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say about the Jones-Klenow method?", "options": ["A) It is widely used to compare the economic growth across countries.", "B) It revolutionizes the way of measuring ordinary people's livelihood.", "C) It focuses on people's consumption rather than their average income.", "D) It is a more comprehensive measure of people's economic well-being."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about the Jones-Klenow method? 问的是「关于这个方\n法作者说了什么」，对象单一、答案在介绍该方法的段落中，属细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say about the Jones-Klenow method? 与上一题同构，say\nabout =「就……谈了什么」，结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】While by no means perfect, it is considerably more comprehensive than average income,\ntaking into account not only growth in consumption per person but also changes in working\ntime, life expectancy, and inequality.\n这一方法虽远非完美，但比平均收入要全面得多——它不仅考虑人均消费的增长，还考虑工\n作时间、预期寿命和不平等程度的变化。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文仅说该方法 can be used to assess economic performance both across\ncountries and over time（可被用来评估），并未说它 is widely used to compare\nthe economic growth across countries（已被广泛使用）。从「可被使用」推到「已\n被广泛使用」是六级阅读典型的过度推断。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说它比平均收入更全面，没有任何句子表明它 revolutionizes the way of\nmeasuring ordinary people's livelihood（彻底变革了衡量方式）。revolutionizes 是\n极强的褒义动词，原文不存在如此强度的评价。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文确实提到该方法考虑 growth in consumption per person，但只是若干维度之\n一，并未称该方法 focuses on people's consumption rather than their average\nincome——把多维方法窄化为「只看消费、不看收入」，是典型以偏概全。\nD) ✓ 正确\nconsiderably more comprehensive than average income ↔\na more comprehensive measure of people's economic well-being\n预判结果 = 更全面的衡量方法，选项 a more comprehensive measure of people's\neconomic well-being 几乎逐词复现预判，D 即正确项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do Jones and Klenow think of the comparison between France and the U. S. in terms of real consumption per person?", "options": ["A) It reflected the existing big gap between the two economies.", "B) It neglected many important indicators of people's welfare.", "C) It covered up the differences between individual citizens.", "D) It failed to count in their difference in natural resources."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do Jones and Klenow think of the comparison ... 指向 Jones 和 Klenow 对\n某次具体对比的看法，对象具体、范围窄，属细节题。\n【拆句】What do Jones and Klenow think of the comparison between France and the U. S. in\nterms of real consumption per person? 主干是 think of the comparison，between\nFrance and the U.S. 修饰 comparison，in terms of real consumption per person 进一\n步限定比较维度。in terms of =「就……而言」是六级高频短语，须认得。\n【定位】However, that comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and\neconomic inequality. ... Because of these differences, comparing France's consumption with\nthe U.S.'s overstates the gap in economic welfare.\n然而，那种比较忽略了其他相关因素：闲暇时间、预期寿命和经济不平等。……由于这些差\n异，仅拿法国和美国的消费做比较，会夸大两国在经济福祉方面的差距。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说仅看消费的对比 overstates the gap（夸大差距），而非 reflected the\nexisting big gap（如实反映了大差距）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthat comparison omits other relevant factors: leisure time, life expectancy, and economic\ninequality\n↔ neglected many important indicators of people's welfare\noverstates the gap in economic welfare ↔\nneglected many important indicators of people's welfare\n预判与选项 neglected many important indicators of people's welfare 完全同向，B\n即正确项。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 between countries（法美两国）的差距被夸大，选项 covered up the\ndifferences between individual citizens 把比较对象偷换成 individual citizens（个体\n公民），属偷换概念。\nD) 无中生有\n原文列举的被忽视因素是 leisure time, life expectancy, and economic inequality，并\n没有提到 difference in natural resources（自然资源差异）——选项虚构了一个原文\n不存在的维度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is an advantage of the Jones-Klenow method?", "options": ["A) It can accurately pinpoint a country's current economic problems.", "B) It can help to raise people's awareness of their economic well-being.", "C) It can diagnose the causes of a country's slowing pace of economic improvement.", "D) It can compare a country's economic conditions between different periods of time."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is an advantage of the Jones-Klenow method? 问的是该方法的一个具体优点，对\n象单一，属细节题。\n【拆句】What is an advantage of the Jones-Klenow method? 简单 wh- 疑问句，无嵌套。注意\nan advantage 用不定冠词，暗示「优点不止一个，你找其中一个能匹配选项的即可」。\n【定位】The Jones-Klenow measure can also assess an economy's performance over time.\nJones-Klenow 这种衡量方法还能评估一个经济体在不同时期的表现。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说能 assess an economy's performance，并未说能 accurately pinpoint a\ncountry's current economic problems（精确指出当前问题）。从「评估表现」到\n「精确诊断当前问题」是几步跳跃。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未提及该方法 raise people's awareness of their economic well-being（提高人\n们对自身福祉的认识）——这是选项凭空添加的功能。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 the pace of improvement has slowed markedly（改善速度明显放缓）是该方法\n的一项观察结果，并非「该方法能诊断减速原因」。选项 diagnose the causes of a\ncountry's slowing pace 把「发现现象」偷换为「诊断原因」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nassess an economy's performance over time ↔\ncompare a country's economic conditions between different periods of time\nboth across countries and over time ↔ between different periods of time\n预判「能评估跨时间表现」即选项 compare a country's economic conditions\nbetween different periods of time 的等值改写，D 即正确项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can we infer from the passage about American people's economic well-being?", "options": ["A) It is much better than that of their European counterparts.", "B) It has been on the decline ever since the turn of the century.", "C) It has not improved as much as reported by the Census Bureau.", "D) It has not been accurately assessed and reported since mid-2000s."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What can we infer from the passage about American people's economic well-\nbeing? 中的 infer 是推断题的硬标志，答案需在原文句意上做一步推演。\n【拆句】What can we infer from the passage about American people's economic well-being?\n主干 What can we infer，about American people's economic well-being 修饰 infer 的\n对象。注意 infer from the passage = 从文中可以推断出，是推断题的高频固定表达。\n【定位】Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve. However, the pace of\nimprovement has slowed markedly.\n自 2007 年以来，美国的经济福祉持续改善。然而，改善的速度明显放缓。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文确实拿美国与法国比较人均消费 real consumption per person in France was\nonly 60% as high as the U.S.，但作者随即点明这种比较 overstates the gap，并未支\n持「美国福祉远好于欧洲国家」。把比较对象由「人均消费」张冠李戴到「整体福祉」\n是典型陷阱。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文明确说 Since 2007, economic welfare in the U.S. has continued to improve，\n福祉一直在改善，仅是速度放缓；选项 on the decline ever since the turn of the\ncentury（自世纪之交以来一直下滑）与原文方向完全相反，且时间起点 the turn of\nthe century 也比原文 Since 2007 早出几年。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe pace of improvement has slowed markedly ↔\nhas not improved as much as reported by the Census Bureau\nUnfortunately, that conclusion puts too much weight on a useful, but flawed and incomplete,\nstatistic.\n↔ reported by the Census Bureau\nP2 表明人口普查局的乐观报告基于有缺陷的统计，P7 又指出实际改善速度明显放缓\n——两端相减即得「实际不如所报告的乐观」，与 has not improved as much as\nreported by the Census Bureau 完全同向。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有任何句子表明美国经济福祉 has not been accurately assessed and reported\nsince mid-2000s（自 2000 年代中期以来未被准确评估和报告）——本文只批评了\n2015 年的人口普查局报告，从未给出「自中前期以来一直未被准确评估」这种宽幅\n断言。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about people making decisions?", "options": ["A) They may become exhausted by making too many decisions for themselves.", "B) They are more cautious in making decisions for others than for themselves.", "C) They tend to make decisions the way they think advantageous to them.", "D) They show considerable differences in their decision-making abilities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about people making decisions? 直接问作者就\"做决定\n的人\"说了哪一项内容,是要原文有出处的陈述题,不是 imply/infer,所以判为细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say about people making decisions? 是一个标准的 \"What does\nX say about Y\" 句型,主干是 \"the author say\",about 短语限定话题为 \"people making\ndecisions\";其中 making decisions 是 people 的现在分词后置定语,等于 \"people who\nmake decisions\",无陷阱。\n【定位】Our own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day causing\nindecision or poor choices, but choosing on behalf of someone else is an enjoyable task that\ndoesn't suffer the same pitfalls.\n我们自己做决定的能力会在一天当中逐渐被耗尽,导致犹豫不决或选择不当;但替别人做选择\n却是件愉悦的差事,不会陷入同样的困境。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nOur own decision-making abilities can become depleted over the course of the day ↔\nThey may become exhausted by making too many decisions for themselves\nover the course of the day ↔ by making too many decisions for themselves\n预判\"决策能力被耗尽\",四个选项里只有 exhausted by making too many decisions 同\n时命中\"耗尽\"和\"反复做决定\"两个要点,直接锁定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说替别人做决定 \"enjoyable\"/\"doesn't suffer the same pitfalls\",并未说替别人做\n决定更谨慎;反过来,P5 还说替别人做决定常包含 riskier choices,与 more cautious in\nmaking decisions for others 完全相反。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 make decisions the way they think advantageous to them 谈\"按自认有利的方\n式做决定\",文中既未讨论\"自利动机\",也未把决策方式描述为\"自认对自己有利\";P3 讲医生\n开抗生素只是\"图省事\",而非\"对自己有利\"。\nD) 无中生有\n原文 P1 强调的是 \"自己 vs 替别人\" 两种角色的差异,不是\"人与人之间能力差异\";选项\nshow considerable differences in their decision-making abilities 把人际差异塞了进\n来,文中无对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the example about the physicians illustrate?", "options": ["A) Patients seldom receive due care towards the end of the day.", "B) Prescription of antibiotics can be harmful to patients' health.", "C) Decision fatigue may prevent people making wise decisions.", "D) Medical doctors are especially susceptible to decision fatigue."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the example about the physicians illustrate? 直接问 example ...\nillustrate(说明)什么,典型例证目的题问法。\n【拆句】What does the example about the physicians illustrate? 主干 \"What does the\nexample illustrate\",about the physicians 是后置介词短语修饰 example,指明是哪一个例\n子;句式平实,无陷阱。\n【定位】Physicians who have been on the job for several hours, for example, are more likely to\nprescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so. \"Presumably it's because it's simple\nand easy to write a prescription and consider a patient case closed rather than investigate\nfurther,\" Polman says.\n比如说,已经连续工作好几个小时的医生,在不合理的情况下也更倾向于给患者开抗生素。\nPolman 解释道:\"大概是因为开一张处方、把病例当作结案处理,比进一步检查要简单省事\n得多。\"\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文 Physicians who have been on the job for several hours 只说\"工作几个小时后\n的医生\"会受影响,并没有说所有 patients 都\"在 day end 时得不到 due care\";选项\nPatients seldom receive due care towards the end of the day 把例子里医生的一种\n不当行为扩大成\"病人普遍得不到照顾\"。\nB) 答非所问\n原文确实提到抗生素被不合理开出,但例子要论证的不是\"抗生素有害\";Prescription of\nantibiotics can be harmful to patients' health 把焦点放在医学结论上,与 Q52 \"the\nexample illustrate(说明什么)\" 的设问方向无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\nare more likely to prescribe antibiotics to patients when it's unwise to do so ↔\nDecision fatigue may prevent people making wise decisions\ndecision fatigue ↔ Decision fatigue\nsimple and easy ... rather than investigate further ↔ prevent people making wise decisions\n预判\"决策疲劳让人放弃理性选择\",Decision fatigue may prevent people making\nwise decisions 是唯一在术语层(decision fatigue)和评价层(wise/unwise)同时对上的\n选项。\nD) 以偏概全\n例子里医生只是体现\"决策疲劳\"的一个普遍现象,不是说医生\"特别\"易受决策疲\n劳;Medical doctors are especially susceptible to decision fatigue 中 especially 把\n医生群体单独拎出来,原文无此区别对待。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "When do people feel less decision fatigue?", "options": ["A) When they take decision shortcuts.", "B) When they help others to make decisions.", "C) When they have major decisions to make.", "D) When they have advisers to turn to."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】When do people feel less decision fatigue? 问的是一个具体条件,原文必有明确出处,不需\n推断,判为细节题。\n【拆句】When do people feel less decision fatigue? 是 When 引导的特殊疑问句,主干 \"people\nfeel ... fatigue\",feel 后接形容词比较级 less 修饰 decision fatigue;less 是定量比较词,关\n注\"变少了多少/在哪种条件下变少\",无陷阱。\n【定位】But decision fatigue goes away when you are making the decision for someone else.\n但是,当你在替别人做决定时,决策疲劳就消失了。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 they feel less tired and rely less on decision shortcuts 明确说替别人做决定\n时\"少用决策捷径\",而选项 When they take decision shortcuts 说\"用捷径时疲劳减轻\",\n方向完全相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwhen you are making the decision for someone else ↔\nWhen they help others to make decisions\nBy taking upon the role of adviser rather than decision maker, one does not suffer the\nconsequences of decision fatigue\n↔ When they help others to make decisions\n预判\"替别人做决定时\",四个选项里只有 When they help others to make decisions\n把\"替别人/帮别人\"这一条件原样保留下来,直接锁定 B。\nC) 无中生有\nWhen they have major decisions to make 谈\"做重大决定时\"疲劳减轻,但原文从未\n把\"决定的重要程度(major)\"和疲劳挂钩;主轴是\"为自己还是为别人\",与重要性无关。\nD) 张冠李戴\nWhen they have advisers to turn to 谈\"有顾问可求助时\"疲劳减轻,但原文里 advisor\n是说\"自己扮演顾问角色\"(taking upon the role of adviser),而不是\"有顾问可找\"——主\n语对象被偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What are people likely to do when decision fatigue sets in?", "options": ["A) They turn to physicians for advice.", "B) They tend to make risky decisions.", "C) They adopt a totally new perspective.", "D) They refrain from trying anything new."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What are people likely to do when decision fatigue sets in? 在问\"做什么\",对应原文具\n体陈述,无 infer/imply 信号,判为细节题。\n【拆句】What are people likely to do when decision fatigue sets in? 主干 \"What are people\nlikely to do\",when 引导时间状语从句限定条件;sets in 是不及物动词短语,意为\"(疲劳等)开\n始出现/到来\",考生若把 sets in 误解为 \"建立/设定\" 会偏题。\n【定位】When people experience decision fatigue—when they are tired of making choices—they have\na tendency to choose to go with the status quo, but the status quo can be problematic, since a\nchange in the course of action can sometimes be important and lead to a positive outcome.\n当人们遭遇决策疲劳——也就是厌烦了做选择时——他们倾向于维持现状,但维持现状可能\n成问题,因为改变行动路线有时候很重要、能带来好结果。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文 P3 出现 physicians 只是举例说明决策疲劳后果,并未说人疲劳了\"会去找医生求\n助\";They turn to physicians for advice 把例子里的角色拉出来当成行为对象,文中\n无据。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 they have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo 说疲劳时倾向维持\n现状(保守),riskier choices 是\"替别人决定\"时才会的;选项 They tend to make risky\ndecisions 把方向反过来,直接搞反。\nC) 无中生有\nThey adopt a totally new perspective 谈\"采纳全新视角\",原文里 fresh perspective\n是\"听别人意见\"带来的好处(offers a fresh perspective),不是疲劳后的行为;选项把好处\n当成行为粘到错条件上。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthey have a tendency to choose to go with the status quo ↔\nThey refrain from trying anything new\ndecision fatigue assuredly leads to inaction ↔ They refrain from trying anything new\n预判\"倾向不动/维持现状\",They refrain from trying anything new 用 refrain from 表\n达\"克制不做\",再加 anything new 强化\"不做新事\",与 status quo + inaction 完美对应,\n锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the passage say about taking some risk in decision making?", "options": ["A) It is vital for one to reach the goal desired.", "B) It is likely to entail serious consequences.", "C) It will enable people to be more creative.", "D) It will more often than not end in regret."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about taking some risk in decision making? 用 \"the\npassage say about\" 问明确陈述,原文有直接出处,判为细节题。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about taking some risk in decision making? 主干 \"What\ndoes the passage say\",about 介词短语带出宾语 taking some risk;in decision making\n是后置介词短语进一步限定 risk 发生的场景,层次清楚、无陷阱。\n【定位】In order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost\nalways essential.\n为了取得成功的结果或回报,某种程度的冒险几乎总是必不可少的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nIn order to achieve a successful outcome or reward, some level of risk is almost always essential\n↔ It is vital for one to reach the goal desired\nsome level of risk ↔ It\n预判\"冒险对成功必需\",It is vital for one to reach the goal desired 用\nvital(=essential)+ reach the goal desired(=achieve outcome/reward)双重命中,锁\n定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文是正面肯定冒险的价值,选项 It is likely to entail serious consequences 却说冒\n险\"很可能带来严重后果\",方向相反;原文 That's not to say that risk is always good 虽\n提醒不是冒险都好,但远不到\"严重后果\"这个负面定性。\nC) 无中生有\nIt will enable people to be more creative 谈\"冒险让人更有创造力\",但原文从未把 risk\n与 creativity/creative 挂钩,文中讲的是 risk→outcome/reward,不是 risk→creativity。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 the possible chagrin of a decision maker who might otherwise prefer a new\ncourse but is unfortunately hindered 是说\"不冒险/不作为\"的人会懊恼;选项 It will\nmore often than not end in regret 却说\"冒险\"会以后悔收场,方向完全颠倒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2018-06-1"});
